00:08:50.835 --> 00:08:52.835
a lot of people, they do have

00:08:54.195 --> 00:08:59.955
they have taken ownership of the quality of the water in the lake, and they're out there doing above and beyond

00:09:00.195 --> 00:09:01.875
what the regulations require.

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:07.170
And so that's a good thing too. And that's really what we're looking for. We would like the people around the lake

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:07.890
to

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:10.770
do the supplementary voluntary

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:12.050
actions to

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:13.490
get the lake better.

00:09:15.935 --> 00:09:16.655
And these

00:09:16.895 --> 00:09:22.335
regulations and the actions that we'd ask people to do will make the lake cleaner and healthier.

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:24.495
And that's what we're all looking for.

00:09:25.455 --> 00:09:29.855
How do we get contaminants into the lake? The rain comes down, it

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:32.500
can pick up contaminants

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.220
through the atmosphere as it's fallen to the earth,

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.140
and it either goes into the lake or it goes onto

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.580
the land and runs off into the lake, and that gives

00:09:42.925 --> 00:09:45.805
even more opportunity to pick up additional

00:09:45.805 --> 00:09:46.765
contaminants.

00:09:47.085 --> 00:09:53.325
The ones we're looking at most here are the phosphate and the nitrogen, which help the weeds grow. The

00:09:55.005 --> 00:09:59.290
other contributor to contaminants in the lake is from the septic systems. And

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:01.450
most people

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:06.170
that that I think might know something about the lake and contamination,

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.810
they think that the septic systems

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.010
are probably the major contributor

00:10:12.145 --> 00:10:12.945
to

00:10:13.345 --> 00:10:17.985
what's going on in the lake. I don't have any percentage what that is over the

00:10:18.305 --> 00:10:20.145
stormwater runoff, but

00:10:20.945 --> 00:10:28.170
septic is an item of concern. And certainly the Alma and Alcoa folks were most interested in what we were doing there.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:32.330
It's interesting, did review

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.530
Columbia's

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:37.210
regulations to see how they compared with what we were doing.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.570
And Columbia didn't tackle the septic system issue.

00:10:42.625 --> 00:10:52.145
I haven't seen John Felleny to ask why not. I don't know if that was a problem over there. They could be on sewers for all I know. I don't know.

00:10:52.865 --> 00:10:53.665
The

00:10:54.465 --> 00:10:56.945
words we put in on septic systems

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.520
are our own words in,

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:03.320
so that's that.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.720
And so I'm

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:06.840
thinking that maybe

