If you walk around

00:34:00.035 --> 00:34:02.035
the lake, might see one down

00:34:02.835 --> 00:34:06.515
across from where Kurt Dowling lives on the lakeside

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:13.680
the Lake Road there. But it's just a little depression in the ground where the rain can collect. They put some

00:34:14.960 --> 00:34:19.440
plants in there and those plants are supposed to help absorb the water

00:34:19.635 --> 00:34:23.155
out of the ground so that it's dry and sucks up a lot of rain.

00:34:23.395 --> 00:34:25.155
But it's just a depression

00:34:25.395 --> 00:34:27.875
that you would put in there. So that's

00:34:28.195 --> 00:34:33.235
what a rain garden's all about. The one on Lake Road's very small. I don't know that it gets you a lot

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:34.780
of mitigation

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:39.500
effect, but at least it shows you what a rain garden looks like.

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:40.620
This

00:34:41.660 --> 00:34:44.380
one's going into Puget Sound, so Puget Sound.

00:34:47.905 --> 00:34:55.425
>> Well, the next, you know, when we were talking, the last time that Jim and I got together and we were going through our numbers,

00:34:56.305 --> 00:34:59.265
you know, we were talking about what's a detention pond?

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:00.620
And

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:03.420
when we get to the sample calculations

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:04.300
I have,

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:14.540
when John, Jim and I were talking, we thought a detention pond was you excavate out a depression or a trench, can fill it full of rocks

00:35:14.945 --> 00:35:15.665
and

00:35:15.825 --> 00:35:19.985
that would be a detention pond and that sort of thing.

00:35:21.265 --> 00:35:22.225
When I look

00:35:22.785 --> 00:35:23.665
online,

00:35:23.665 --> 00:35:24.625
for examples,

00:35:25.665 --> 00:35:27.585
the detention pond

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:32.140
can either be a dry detention pond or a wet detention pond, and it's just a

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:35.660
rather significant sized depression that

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:38.380
collects the rain when it goes into it.

00:35:39.955 --> 00:35:44.435
And your storm drains, your runoff can run down in here, it collects it, allows it to

00:35:45.315 --> 00:35:47.155
soak into the water.

00:35:47.395 --> 00:35:51.475
So that's when I look online to see what a detention pond is,

00:35:51.955 --> 00:35:53.155
that's what I came up with there.

00:35:58.120 --> 00:36:01.080
The next thing that we talked about and I'm really,

00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:03.560
this is my favorite now,

00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:05.160
is a dry well.

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:08.505
And so there's

00:36:08.505 --> 00:36:09.145
people

00:36:09.305 --> 00:36:09.945
that,

00:36:10.665 --> 00:36:20.105
you know, they take the runoff from the roofs, run it down through a gutter, run the gutters to a downspout and into a dry well. And there's a couple of different ways you can do that.

00:36:21.510 --> 00:36:24.870
But what a lot of people have are just these things.

00:36:25.350 --> 00:36:26.870
And I don't know if anybody

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:29.430
knows what a Quonset hut is, but

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:33.270
they are the old temporary buildings during World War II that

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:35.350
are just kind of semicircular

00:36:35.350 --> 00:36:36.230
metal

00:36:37.225 --> 00:36:39.865
that you put two ends on and people

00:36:39.945 --> 00:36:55.560
lived in did all kinds of things there. Well, this looks like a little quonset hut that you put in the ground and you run your downspout into it and it's sitting on a bunch of crushed rock there, but it gives a spot for all the water to go and it slowly sinks into the ground.

00:36:55.960 --> 00:37:00.360
And these can be covered up, grass grown over it, you don't even know that it's there.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:02.440
Another

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.200
example of a dry well that I saw online looking,

00:37:06.835 --> 00:37:09.395
you would dig a big cylindrical

00:37:09.395 --> 00:37:11.875
hole into the ground and you would line it with

00:37:12.515 --> 00:37:13.795
the cinder blocks.

00:37:14.595 --> 00:37:15.395
Again,

00:37:15.955 --> 00:37:17.715
the storm water runs into

00:37:17.955 --> 00:37:20.515
the dry well, it soaks into the ground

00:37:20.820 --> 00:37:21.620
through

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:26.340
the holes and all the cinder blocks and it disperses that way.

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:29.380
And two things that all this

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:35.860
rainwater mitigation does for us is not only does it keep the pollutants from going into the lake,

00:37:36.815 --> 00:37:37.535
it

00:37:37.695 --> 00:37:42.095
helps keep our water table healthy. So it goes down into the ground,

00:37:42.575 --> 00:37:44.735
the pollutants are attacked

00:37:44.735 --> 00:37:52.110
by the bacteria that are in the ground, so that gets rid of that. And the water sinks down into the water table and maintains

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:55.630
a healthy environment for all our wells around the lake.

00:37:56.030 --> 00:37:57.950
So anyhow, those are examples

00:37:57.950 --> 00:38:02.555
of what we're looking at for these different mitigation areas here.

00:38:07.195 --> 00:38:10.715
Now we're gonna head back to the end of the

00:38:11.995 --> 00:38:12.955
worksheet

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:19.940
and what does the answer mean? And so we said what we were going to do is take 2,100

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:21.460
square feet of lawn

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:24.180
and run it into,

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:28.100
equivalent from a one half inch rain event

00:38:29.925 --> 00:38:30.725
into

00:38:30.725 --> 00:38:32.165
a detention pond.

00:38:32.485 --> 00:38:33.925
And so the first

00:38:34.245 --> 00:38:34.885
question

00:38:35.365 --> 00:38:38.085
we need to ask is how much is

00:38:38.565 --> 00:38:41.205
a onetwo inches of rain on 2,100

00:38:41.205 --> 00:38:42.165
square feet of lawn?

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:44.350
And so you multiply

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:51.310
the 2,100 square feet times a half inch of rain with a conversion factor, one foot is 12 inches,

00:38:51.550 --> 00:38:57.495
and that comes up to 88 cubic feet of rainwater. That's what we've got to collect, that's what we've got to mitigate.

00:39:00.615 --> 00:39:02.535
Then we also said we were gonna

00:39:02.775 --> 00:39:06.055
take that two inches of runoff from the roof.

00:39:07.415 --> 00:39:11.420
So I took 400 square feet of roof times two inches of rain,

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:14.140
again times the conversion

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:16.540
factor to get it all into feet,

00:39:16.860 --> 00:39:19.340
and that comes out to 67 cubic feet

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:22.300
of runoff water. Add these two things together,

00:39:22.985 --> 00:39:25.225
we're looking to mitigate 155

00:39:25.225 --> 00:39:26.905
cubic feet of rainwater.

00:39:27.945 --> 00:39:30.425
So that's the amount that we've got to do something with.

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:37.920
Now I got myself into the

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:42.320
trench because I started working backwards from

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:46.400
the line in our regulations

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.360
that said if we were gonna do 100

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:50.640
square feet or less

00:39:51.175 --> 00:39:53.095
of added roofing

00:39:54.375 --> 00:39:56.455
or patio or whatever,

00:39:57.895 --> 00:39:59.495
we didn't need to do the

00:39:59.975 --> 00:40:03.575
worksheet. We could just dig a trench and fill it full of stone.

00:40:04.820 --> 00:40:07.540
So I said okay, and I asked

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:08.580
what's

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:09.300
a

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:15.540
retention

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:18.180
basin? And they said well that's

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:19.540
this, you just make it bigger.

00:40:20.655 --> 00:40:21.455
And

00:40:22.735 --> 00:40:24.335
I said, okay, well under

00:40:25.135 --> 00:40:31.775
the shed we will excavate down so that we can put a bunch of crushed stone in to get the volume to

00:40:32.175 --> 00:40:34.415
accommodate this 155

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:37.270
cubic feet of water that we're looking to store.

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:40.870
If fill

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:43.030
the excavated

00:40:43.030 --> 00:40:45.350
storage reservoir with crushed stone,

00:40:46.630 --> 00:40:50.230
we figure we reduce the volume by about half.

00:40:51.175 --> 00:40:53.415
So if we want to get 155

00:40:53.415 --> 00:40:54.855
cubic feet of water,

00:40:55.335 --> 00:41:01.735
we need to have three ten cubic feet of storage reservoir filled with stone so that we can accommodate that.

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:06.400
I looked at the stone pad area

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:12.240
for the shed being about one foot larger than the roof, so it goes all the way around that we put the shed on.

00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:15.520
So I took 22 feet by 22 feet,

00:41:16.115 --> 00:41:19.795
and that comes out to four eighty four square feet area

00:41:19.795 --> 00:41:22.275
of the stone pad that we're going to put the shed on.

00:41:24.035 --> 00:41:30.275
Then I said, well, just to make the math easy, if we dig the storage reservoir nine inches deep,

00:41:31.070 --> 00:41:32.430
let's see what we get.

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:37.950
So I took my four eighty four square feet times threefour foot

00:41:39.790 --> 00:41:46.190
because that put the nine inches to 12 inches conversion factor in there for me and it comes up with three sixty three cubic feet.

00:41:46.845 --> 00:41:52.925
Bigger than three ten, so we're happy. We're storing enough water underneath the shed.

00:41:54.685 --> 00:42:03.085
And so that's what Jim has to do when he's going out in the field to work with the guys to implement the improvements that we came up with by

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:04.010
using the worksheet.

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:05.930
There

00:42:05.930 --> 00:42:07.050
might be places

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:08.890
if

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:12.330
we didn't want to put all the water under the shed,

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:15.050
maybe there's places

00:42:15.370 --> 00:42:19.575
like on the side of your driveway. I've got on my driveway where

00:42:19.575 --> 00:42:22.215
water comes running down like crazy,

00:42:22.535 --> 00:42:26.215
runs down a hill and into the lake. Well, would be a good place to put

00:42:27.335 --> 00:42:29.975
a storage reservoir, dry well, something like that.

00:42:30.535 --> 00:42:36.320
So that's up to Jim working with the applicants to figure out where's the best place and the best method

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:37.280
to

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:39.440
mitigate the water runoff.

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:43.680
>> So the whole area under the shed is the reservoir.

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:50.295
>> Right. In this case, and I thought that would work. I know the last time we were going through this, there was some concern that it

00:42:51.495 --> 00:42:56.375
might rot out the timbers that you're sitting the shed on. And my thought was, well, we dig it.

00:42:57.735 --> 00:43:06.990
Stack the stone a couple of inch higher than the surrounding area so that the water would never get up that high, you can protect it. But again, a lot of different answers,

00:43:07.310 --> 00:43:10.670
this one seemed to work pretty good for this particular thing,

00:43:11.310 --> 00:43:14.030
especially when the applicant was looking at putting the crushed

00:43:14.110 --> 00:43:16.110
stone bed down to put the shed on anyhow.

00:43:17.635 --> 00:43:23.315
So that's how we use the worksheet to come up with the mitigation for the stormwater runoff.

00:43:24.035 --> 00:43:26.355
Any other questions on that process?

