what are the best fertilizers

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:13.880
to use if people need to because I can't give that.

00:50:16.355 --> 00:50:24.675
If John, you're still with us there, one of the things that became clear to me is I learned a lot living on the lake that I didn't know about

00:50:25.075 --> 00:50:28.035
helping the lake is we need a

00:50:29.320 --> 00:50:35.160
education process for the folks that live around the lake. So when we send out that semiannual newsletter,

00:50:35.400 --> 00:50:38.280
maybe we ought to have a sheet that goes in that with voluntary

00:50:38.760 --> 00:50:39.560
actions

00:50:39.560 --> 00:50:41.080
we can take and start

00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:42.200
educating

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:43.800
the folks

00:50:44.095 --> 00:50:46.495
on what they can do to help out. Are

00:50:46.495 --> 00:50:49.455
there people at the lake that are fertilizing?

00:50:49.775 --> 00:50:51.295
>> There's a couple, yeah.

00:50:52.575 --> 00:50:54.655
Yes, the answer is yes. And

00:50:56.335 --> 00:50:58.895
there's nothing we can do about it from a regulation point of view.

00:51:01.930 --> 00:51:05.370
We can't go around and be the fertilizer police. We just have to

00:51:05.850 --> 00:51:07.450
appeal to people's

00:51:09.770 --> 00:51:12.330
better natures to go out there and

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:20.195
not put fertilizer on it. You look at my yard, you can tell that I do not put fertilizer on my grass.

00:51:20.675 --> 00:51:24.115
It looks like it. Totally agree. You can have that be the badge of honor.

00:51:24.995 --> 00:51:30.195
>> This is John. I totally agree with what you just said. Okay. Thanks, John. Yep.

00:51:30.880 --> 00:51:32.400
Sending out a letter

00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:34.160
to Lake

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:34.800
Lake

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:38.240
District people

00:51:38.240 --> 00:51:39.600
to not

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:42.000
use fertilizer and the reasons why.

00:51:42.495 --> 00:51:43.695
Yep. And

00:51:44.255 --> 00:51:52.015
>> maybe when you find this person Jed that's gonna help you with what you can use to green up your lawn, maybe there is something that

00:51:52.415 --> 00:51:54.255
they can use that

00:51:55.030 --> 00:51:56.390
won't harm the lake.

00:51:58.230 --> 00:51:58.950
>> Maybe,

00:51:59.750 --> 00:52:01.190
Jose, I don't know.

00:52:02.950 --> 00:52:04.310
One of the persons

00:52:04.550 --> 00:52:05.590
that I was,

00:52:06.550 --> 00:52:07.350
who will

00:52:07.835 --> 00:52:09.835
remain anonymous here, but

00:52:10.475 --> 00:52:16.075
I know she's very enthused about keeping the lake clean and she said she does fertilize her yard.

00:52:16.235 --> 00:52:18.715
I said, what are you doing that for? I said, well

00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:20.870
I use this

00:52:21.190 --> 00:52:24.950
organic stuff that they say is lake friendly.

00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:27.510
And I said, come on.

00:52:28.150 --> 00:52:34.315
And she said, I know if it makes the yard green, it makes the lake green too. And that's where I got that light from.

00:52:36.155 --> 00:52:36.875
And so

00:52:37.915 --> 00:52:41.355
we just got to reduce that as much as we can.

00:52:43.515 --> 00:52:46.555
We're going to move into septic system at this point.

00:52:53.710 --> 00:52:55.070
Again, this

00:52:56.190 --> 00:52:58.510
was not in the Columbia regulations.

00:52:58.510 --> 00:53:00.910
I don't know where this exactly came from,

00:53:02.755 --> 00:53:06.675
but I know it is true that the Lake District has a lot of small lots

00:53:06.835 --> 00:53:08.915
with on-site well and septic,

00:53:09.395 --> 00:53:11.315
and it makes it hard to

00:53:13.955 --> 00:53:17.235
put your septic tank exactly where you would like to.

00:53:18.800 --> 00:53:20.800
And one of the things that,

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:24.400
you know, I had sent out an email to

00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:26.800
Thad King, who's the

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:28.000
sanitarian

00:53:28.000 --> 00:53:28.720
for

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:34.575
Eastern Highlands Health District, asking him a couple of questions and I hadn't got back from him yet.

00:53:36.895 --> 00:53:40.495
But we'll get there in a minute on couple of issues

00:53:41.135 --> 00:53:42.335
with that. But

00:53:42.655 --> 00:53:47.630
when I emailed him the first time asking him how far from the wetlands

00:53:47.710 --> 00:53:49.710
the septic tank needed to be,

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:53.150
his answer was, well, we don't regulate

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:54.590
for the wetlands.

00:53:55.070 --> 00:53:57.710
We require 50 feet from water.

00:53:58.705 --> 00:54:02.065
And then if it's one of these old small lake lots,

00:54:02.065 --> 00:54:03.905
the number is 25 feet.

00:54:04.065 --> 00:54:06.465
So that's

00:54:06.465 --> 00:54:08.465
what I know about that at this point.

00:54:09.345 --> 00:54:12.865
The majority of the septic systems were installed prior to 1980.

00:54:14.750 --> 00:54:16.270
I thought that

00:54:16.270 --> 00:54:19.230
that probably meant that they were starting to get old.

00:54:20.990 --> 00:54:22.110
I do know,

00:54:22.430 --> 00:54:23.630
and we haven't got

00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:26.190
in here

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:28.750
how to take credit for it, is

00:54:29.415 --> 00:54:31.575
that septic tank

00:54:32.695 --> 00:54:33.655
design,

00:54:33.735 --> 00:54:37.415
there have been a lot of improvements in the design of septic systems

00:54:37.655 --> 00:54:39.495
over the last several years.

00:54:40.135 --> 00:54:44.550
And for instance, what used to be a three bedroom cottage on the lake,

00:54:44.710 --> 00:54:54.310
you can now make it a year round because you can get an approved septic system down on the small lot there. So I know the septic systems of today are much better than what they used to be.

00:54:56.585 --> 00:55:03.065
We say septic systems are undersized, not meet the minimum separating distance to groundwater required to renovate

00:55:03.065 --> 00:55:04.745
septic effluent

00:55:05.465 --> 00:55:12.500
residential waste. And I'll talk about that more on the next slide where I've got a little picture in there. I got schooled on that recently.

00:55:14.980 --> 00:55:19.220
Existing septic systems are often located in the water table. That's not good.

00:55:19.860 --> 00:55:23.780
And the new lake regs would apply only to zone A and B for the septic systems.

00:55:24.555 --> 00:55:25.595
>> In the ground

00:55:25.915 --> 00:55:30.795
to water, what was that that we put in the regulations? Was it 12 inches or 18?

00:55:31.035 --> 00:55:31.755
>> We

00:55:32.315 --> 00:55:34.475
put 12. My recommendation

00:55:34.475 --> 00:55:35.115
is

00:55:35.435 --> 00:55:36.795
that we go with 18

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:39.520
because 18 is what the

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:41.840
health district code is today.

00:55:42.000 --> 00:55:43.920
And so why shouldn't we

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:47.840
expect it to be 18 inches? It makes it a lot easier.

00:55:48.560 --> 00:55:49.200
And so

00:55:49.535 --> 00:55:51.615
my thinking is that we should

00:55:52.015 --> 00:55:56.015
expect somebody to comply with the existing health code standards

00:55:56.015 --> 00:55:58.975
for their septic if they're doing this major renovation.

00:55:59.935 --> 00:56:00.655
Yeah, right.

00:56:02.610 --> 00:56:04.930
>> Okay, so anyhow, found this little

00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:06.130
diagram

00:56:07.730 --> 00:56:08.850
that shows

00:56:09.250 --> 00:56:13.970
the wastewater coming out of the house, goes down into your septic tank, the water runs down,

00:56:14.370 --> 00:56:18.315
and it goes into your leach field here where the

00:56:18.635 --> 00:56:19.195
water

00:56:19.755 --> 00:56:21.995
leaches out into the surrounding

00:56:23.035 --> 00:56:26.155
ground and then the bacteria in the ground can

00:56:27.835 --> 00:56:29.755
eat up all the contaminants

00:56:29.835 --> 00:56:30.635
in there

00:56:30.955 --> 00:56:31.435
and make,

00:56:32.230 --> 00:56:33.430
do away with

00:56:34.230 --> 00:56:36.710
all the septic stuff that's

00:56:36.710 --> 00:56:37.910
coming out of your house,

00:56:38.310 --> 00:56:41.190
and it gets it before it gets down into the lake,

00:56:42.230 --> 00:56:44.470
or down into the groundwater for that matter.

00:56:45.195 --> 00:56:47.195
Because if it gets down to the groundwater,

00:56:47.195 --> 00:56:48.795
then the bacteria doesn't

00:56:49.195 --> 00:56:54.395
eat it up so well, doesn't have time to do that, and it can flow downhill and run into the lake.

00:56:55.195 --> 00:56:57.675
So when we're talking about

00:56:58.155 --> 00:57:00.955
leach fields, we show it being

00:57:00.440 --> 00:57:01.960
above the groundwater.

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:05.000
That's where we want it to be,

00:57:06.840 --> 00:57:07.880
and it's

00:57:08.200 --> 00:57:11.640
possible that some of the septic systems around the lake

00:57:12.200 --> 00:57:14.285
are in the groundwater now,

00:57:15.325 --> 00:57:19.645
especially in the spring and that sort of thing. So that's what we're looking to do.

00:57:21.405 --> 00:57:23.805
Conditions requiring a new septic system.

00:57:24.765 --> 00:57:26.045
Any increase

00:57:26.360 --> 00:57:31.880
in use requiring a B100 approval under the Connecticut Public Health Code, that's what we

00:57:31.880 --> 00:57:37.480
show the health code what we're doing and they determine whether or not we need a new septic system for doing that.

00:57:38.995 --> 00:57:45.715
If we increase the net floor area of a principal building by 20% for zone A or 30% zone B,

00:57:47.075 --> 00:57:48.115
you have to

00:57:48.835 --> 00:57:58.600
upgrade or put in a new septic system. And that's a change I need to make is put to upgrade. Not necessarily a new septic system if

00:57:59.640 --> 00:58:00.440
we can

00:58:00.840 --> 00:58:04.840
get you to where you're as good as the requirements for today,

00:58:05.160 --> 00:58:06.040
that's what we're looking for.

00:58:09.305 --> 00:58:12.105
The next one down is a little different. It talks about

00:58:12.425 --> 00:58:17.465
overall footprint of the principal structure exceeding 25% or 30%

00:58:17.705 --> 00:58:20.025
for all structures. That's where you add a shed

00:58:20.240 --> 00:58:22.720
and it exceeds 40 or 50%

00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:24.960
of what you had before,

00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:27.680
we're going to take a look at your septic system.

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:29.280
I

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:32.640
thought we had it more strict than that.

00:58:33.440 --> 00:58:33.760
Nope,

00:58:36.025 --> 00:58:37.225
that's what was there.

00:58:39.785 --> 00:58:41.305
And again, there's a difference,

00:58:41.545 --> 00:58:43.705
the 20% for Zone A

00:58:43.865 --> 00:58:45.545
on the net floor area,

00:58:48.185 --> 00:58:50.985
the 25% for increasing the overall footprint.

00:58:53.590 --> 00:58:54.550
But that's

00:58:54.790 --> 00:58:57.590
what comes out of the zoning regs that

00:58:58.070 --> 00:58:59.350
we were looking at.

00:59:00.550 --> 00:59:01.270
Additional

00:59:03.910 --> 00:59:04.950
one or more bedrooms.

00:59:07.105 --> 00:59:11.585
And here's where we were talking about when greater than 50% of the principal square front

00:59:12.545 --> 00:59:15.025
principal structures square footage read real.

00:59:15.425 --> 00:59:16.625
It used to say

00:59:16.945 --> 00:59:19.985
and an increase in square footage.

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:23.760
I took the increase in square footage out of there because if you're

00:59:24.240 --> 00:59:26.560
essentially tearing down your house and rebuilding it,

00:59:27.200 --> 00:59:30.080
want the septic tank to be working good.

00:59:31.840 --> 00:59:35.200
And this regulation is cumulative as of the date of adoption.

00:59:44.055 --> 00:59:47.735
I'm going go way back up for a minute because

00:59:48.215 --> 00:59:51.575
all of a sudden it dawned on me one of the things

01:00:06.995 --> 01:00:09.475
And as fast as that thought went in my head,

01:00:10.115 --> 01:00:15.795
it it drifted out again. So I guess, well, I waited too long. That shows,

01:00:16.195 --> 01:00:18.195
that those thoughts, are fleeting.

01:00:18.670 --> 01:00:27.470
Let's

01:00:27.470 --> 01:00:28.830
talk about who

01:00:30.190 --> 01:00:35.405
doesn't need to upgrade their septic system when they're doing all those things on the preceding page.

01:00:36.525 --> 01:00:40.285
And we use the word exemption here, and I know exemption

01:00:40.285 --> 01:00:41.325
is

01:00:42.285 --> 01:00:44.605
a bad word on zoning regs.

01:00:45.470 --> 01:00:46.350
You know,

01:00:47.550 --> 01:00:53.070
there's probably a better word in there. I know that the last time we had our

01:00:54.110 --> 01:00:58.430
lawyer take a look at it, he said, okay, you've got enough

01:00:57.905 --> 01:01:01.825
specifics in there that maybe it's not an exemption. It's just something

01:01:01.985 --> 01:01:05.265
else, but he didn't give me a better word than exemption.

01:01:05.825 --> 01:01:07.425
So we'll call it that for now.

01:01:09.425 --> 01:01:13.345
But you get a Connecticut

01:01:12.330 --> 01:01:14.250
registered professional engineer,

01:01:14.330 --> 01:01:15.530
analyze

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:16.890
your existing system,

01:01:17.610 --> 01:01:19.450
and certify all of the following.

01:01:20.330 --> 01:01:28.235
In the existing system greater than 18 inches above max high water table, that's where our current regulation, Scott, used to say 12.

01:01:28.315 --> 01:01:32.795
I think it needs to be 18. That's what the Connecticut health code is and

01:01:33.195 --> 01:01:34.715
I'm comfortable with that.

01:01:35.035 --> 01:01:35.595
Yep.

01:01:37.755 --> 01:01:42.770
Greater than 35 feet to the high water mark at the Andover Lake or tributary. I'm

01:01:42.770 --> 01:01:43.810
still struggling

01:01:44.370 --> 01:01:46.690
to get a good understanding of this.

01:01:48.290 --> 01:01:48.770
So

01:01:49.890 --> 01:01:52.050
let me go use an example.

01:01:55.490 --> 01:01:56.450
A lady

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:58.770
in one of the small lots

01:01:58.315 --> 01:01:59.035
on

01:01:59.275 --> 01:02:02.395
Lake Road just down the street from me had a

01:02:02.875 --> 01:02:04.635
new septic system put in

01:02:05.195 --> 01:02:06.715
within the last year.

01:02:08.875 --> 01:02:11.035
So the plan that

01:02:11.500 --> 01:02:12.700
was approved

01:02:15.020 --> 01:02:18.300
was keeping it 25 feet from the lake.

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:20.140
And that was

01:02:21.740 --> 01:02:22.620
what was

01:02:23.420 --> 01:02:24.460
requested by

01:02:24.700 --> 01:02:26.460
the Eastern Highland Health District.

01:02:28.075 --> 01:02:28.795
Somehow

01:02:28.955 --> 01:02:30.795
in the review by

01:02:31.035 --> 01:02:31.995
environmental,

01:02:31.995 --> 01:02:33.995
the state DEEP

01:02:33.995 --> 01:02:35.035
or whatever,

01:02:35.515 --> 01:02:38.875
there was some reason, and I'm not sure the technical issue behind it,

01:02:40.210 --> 01:02:44.610
but we ended up putting the septic system in 21 feet from the lake.

01:02:47.650 --> 01:02:50.130
And I didn't understand that. I wasn't

01:02:50.370 --> 01:02:57.075
a civil engineer, wasn't smart on septic systems at the time, know a little bit more, but I still don't know why that

01:02:57.315 --> 01:02:58.355
had to be there.

01:02:58.835 --> 01:03:06.595
>> And I don't know how that jives with 35 feet. Is there a grave problem or what happened that it's so close to the lake?

01:03:07.715 --> 01:03:08.275
>> I

01:03:08.515 --> 01:03:09.235
don't recall.

01:03:09.740 --> 01:03:15.420
And Jim, you might remember a little bit, you might not. All I know is we said that

01:03:15.980 --> 01:03:17.580
the state agreed with it,

01:03:17.980 --> 01:03:22.705
the health district agreed with it. Who were we to disagree with it? And

01:03:23.425 --> 01:03:29.905
in my thought, they were putting a new state of the art septic system in. I thought that was much improved

01:03:29.985 --> 01:03:35.185
over the failed septic that they had in there at the time. So I said, okay, I've got my vote.

01:03:36.550 --> 01:03:39.990
I remember the meeting, the wetlands meeting. Yep.

01:03:43.270 --> 01:03:44.070
>> And so

01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:45.750
I'm looking for some

01:03:46.390 --> 01:03:48.790
help. Kevin, this might be up your alley.

01:03:49.495 --> 01:03:53.095
I don't know if you've got any knowledge on septic systems or not, but

01:03:53.575 --> 01:03:58.935
I struggle with how we can require that when we're putting in brand new septic systems down

01:03:59.495 --> 01:04:01.175
within 25 feet.

01:04:04.135 --> 01:04:04.615
Anyhow,

01:04:05.980 --> 01:04:06.700
I'm

01:04:08.140 --> 01:04:09.980
I'm okay with those words there.

01:04:10.540 --> 01:04:12.060
The last time we were together,

01:04:12.940 --> 01:04:20.035
Eric Anderson was sitting in with us and he's as smart on these regs as anybody is. He was instrumental

01:04:20.035 --> 01:04:22.195
in getting them to where they are now.

01:04:22.435 --> 01:04:25.395
He was comfortable with 35 feet saying, well,

01:04:26.835 --> 01:04:31.635
if you can't get 35 feet, you don't get to build your house any bigger, you know, and

01:04:31.875 --> 01:04:33.420
do any of that. But

01:04:35.260 --> 01:04:36.940
I don't know. I

01:04:38.860 --> 01:04:46.140
>> am not sure about that one yet. Are you sure on the 35 or do you think it should be more than that? I think it should be less than that.

01:04:47.085 --> 01:04:48.045
>> If

01:04:48.285 --> 01:04:48.845
the

01:04:49.485 --> 01:04:50.205
state

01:04:51.005 --> 01:04:55.645
health code requires 25 feet for a small lot on the lake,

01:04:58.045 --> 01:04:59.645
how can we go

01:05:00.210 --> 01:05:04.610
with more than that? And I guess we can because we're trying to protect the lake, but

01:05:05.330 --> 01:05:10.450
I'm not sure that you can get there on these small lots. And maybe that's one where it comes back to a special permit.

01:05:12.605 --> 01:05:18.525
I don't have it. I'm sharing with you folks that I don't have the answer to that. When I go to public hearing, won't

01:05:18.845 --> 01:05:20.765
brag about not knowing the answer.

01:05:23.165 --> 01:05:27.005
But I put that out here in case anybody's got a good thought on that.

01:05:29.430 --> 01:05:31.350
And I'm

01:05:31.350 --> 01:05:36.710
going move on to the last one because that doesn't make sense to me either, but I know it's consistent with the public health code.

01:05:37.430 --> 01:05:39.110
It says exceeds 50%

01:05:39.110 --> 01:05:44.455
of the size requirement for new septic for a new principal building equivalent to the proposed building.

01:05:45.895 --> 01:05:48.295
And I said, I want 100%.

01:05:48.295 --> 01:05:51.255
Why don't we want it to be as big as

01:05:51.495 --> 01:05:53.015
a new septic would be?

01:05:53.735 --> 01:05:56.535
And so I went back to the Connecticut

01:05:56.020 --> 01:05:58.180
state regulations.

01:05:58.660 --> 01:06:00.580
And that's what it says, 50%.

01:06:00.660 --> 01:06:01.220
So

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:07.780
I've asked Thad King for a little help on understanding that one and he hasn't got back to me with the answer yet.

01:06:10.865 --> 01:06:12.145
So I'm not there.

01:06:12.545 --> 01:06:13.505
Don't

01:06:13.505 --> 01:06:14.625
know. That's kind of

01:06:15.185 --> 01:06:17.425
>> odd, isn't it? It is kind of odd.

01:06:18.225 --> 01:06:20.625
I know that that is

01:06:21.160 --> 01:06:22.680
right out of the Connecticut

01:06:22.760 --> 01:06:24.280
state statutes.

01:06:26.600 --> 01:06:31.080
So on the top one and the bottom one are per the Connecticut statutes.

01:06:31.560 --> 01:06:33.160
I'm not sure

01:06:34.045 --> 01:06:40.845
that that's a state statute but I know it's bigger than Eastern Highland Health Districts when they approve a septic tank.

01:06:42.365 --> 01:06:44.045
But that's where we are on that

01:06:45.805 --> 01:06:46.525
as of now.

01:06:48.340 --> 01:06:52.580
I put in some voluntary measures here once again to ask for some help.

01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:57.780
We would expect the people to pump out,

01:06:58.740 --> 01:07:04.645
frequently pump out their septic tanks. The last time I heard that was, we'd like people to do that every three years.

01:07:06.325 --> 01:07:09.605
Consider installation of a septic tank effluent filter.

01:07:10.005 --> 01:07:11.685
Don't know how to do that yet,

